{
  "dom_id": "step-q43178",
  "name": "Question 8",
  "problem": "$1-(-\\frac{3}{2})=$",
  "worked_solution": "To subtract $-\\frac{3}{2}$ from $1$, we drop  the parentheses and combine the two negative signs to make a single plus sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-(-\\frac{3}{2})=1+\\frac{3}{2} & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we express the two numbers as fractions over a common denominator of $2$\n and combine the numerators. This gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+\\frac{3}{2}=\\frac{2}{2}+\\frac{3}{2}=\\frac{2+3}{2} & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we work out $2+3$ using our number line.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14150464/assets/c83beb4c78b8690dc04d2cc95bb20e94e33eac19962edea1055089adb4213ad4.png)\n\nSo we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{2+3}{2}=\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$",
